{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-population-top20-production-v2:2023:BGD:17069","iso3":"BGD","ruler_name":"Sheikh Hasina","score_1_to_10":7.0,"confidence_score":66.0,"plausible_score_range":{"lower":6.0,"upper":8.0},"decisive_positive_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E021","explanation":"A contemporaneous official state position supported peaceful use and verifiable disarmament."},{"evidence_id":"E023","explanation":"The government provided concrete security arrangements for a target-year uranium shipment."}],"decisive_negative_evidence":[],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"Bangladesh inherited safeguards commitments and a Russian-backed plant under construction; sanctions disrupted payments and constrained implementation choices.","ruler_attribution":"Formal responsibility is established through government diplomatic policy, sanctions adaptation, and national security arrangements; shared technical authority reduces weight but does not erase attribution.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E019","explanation":"Construction status does not establish operating safety or effectiveness."},{"evidence_id":"E020","explanation":"Inherited legal commitments do not prove day-to-day enforcement."}],"source_mix":"IAEA legal and program records, an official UN mission statement, Reuters reporting, and domestic news; authoritative for formal acts but official/institutional-source heavy.","structured_prior_summary":"The available local package contains no LF/LS facts or longitudinal signals for 1B; it contributes no score direction.","chapter_rationale":"Overall, Sheikh Hasina’s record reflects generally responsible but incompletely verified existential-risk management, scoring 7.0. Her government maintained a peaceful-use and disarmament position, adapted Rooppur nuclear-plant payments after sanctions disrupted normal channels, and provided army and law-enforcement security for the first uranium shipment (E021–E023). These were real 2023 choices within national executive responsibility, not merely inherited country characteristics. The favorable record is qualified because the two reactors were still under construction, longstanding safeguards and treaty commitments predated the year, and the cited sources do not independently audit day-to-day safety or enforcement (E019–E020). The sanctions workaround demonstrates continuity under pressure, but not exceptional technical performance. No cited evidence shows weaponization, nuclear coercion, or reckless crisis conduct. A higher score is rejected because consequential, independently verified improvement in operational safety is absent; a lower score is rejected because the government made concrete, attributable security and peaceful-use choices rather than relying only on rhetoric.","lower_anchor_rejected":"Concrete shipment security and peaceful-use policy exceed mixed or weak management despite incomplete outcome evidence.","higher_anchor_rejected":"No independently verified operating-safety improvement or durable reduction comparable to the 8–9 anchor is shown.","manual_review_required":false,"manual_review_reason_type":null,"manual_review_reason":"Additional weak areas: 1B.3 command and operational safety outcomes; 1B.6 proliferation enforcement; 1B.7 independent expertise; 1B.9 other catastrophic risks; 1B.10 durable end posture; Partially supported but weak lenses: 1B.3","chapter_specific":[{"field":"chapter_rubric_version","value":"chapter_1b_v5"},{"field":"exposure_profile","value":"limited_exposure_with_choices: civilian nuclear construction, fuel handling, sanctions-related supply exposure, and diplomatic nuclear policy"},{"field":"trajectory","value":"stable-to-modestly safer; project security and continuity advanced, but operational outcomes remain unverified"},{"field":"absolute_scale_classification","value":"generally_responsible_management"}]}
{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-population-top20-production-v2:2023:BRA:16162","iso3":"BRA","ruler_name":"Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva","score_1_to_10":7.0,"confidence_score":67.0,"plausible_score_range":{"lower":6.0,"upper":8.0},"decisive_positive_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E083","explanation":"Lula directly established a national cybersecurity policy and committee."},{"evidence_id":"E076","explanation":"Brazil advanced a formal 2023 disarmament and nonproliferation position."}],"decisive_negative_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E078","explanation":"Lula directly endorsed a risk-bearing nuclear-propulsion program without cited independent outcome evidence."}],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"Lula inherited Brazil’s safeguards regime and nuclear-submarine program; 2023 authority was shared with ministries, military institutions, and safeguards bodies.","ruler_attribution":"Direct for the cybersecurity decree and submarine endorsement; formal governmental responsibility for diplomacy and budgets; safeguards operation is institutionally shared.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E077","explanation":"Funding a nuclear-propulsion submarine increases oversight burdens and does not itself reduce risk."},{"evidence_id":"E079","explanation":"Safeguards review documents process, not exceptional compliance outcomes."}],"source_mix":"Official presidential, ministry, safeguards, and security records with Reuters reporting; good formal-act coverage but limited independent outcome evaluation.","structured_prior_summary":"The available local package contains no LF/LS facts or signals and supplies no score direction.","chapter_rationale":"Overall, Lula’s 2023 record shows generally responsible risk management with a meaningful but incomplete institutional agenda, scoring 7.0. Brazil used formal diplomacy to press disarmament obligations, maintained active bilateral safeguards review, and Lula directly created a national cybersecurity policy and committee (E076, E079, E083). At the same time, he personally endorsed and his government funded the nuclear-propulsion submarine program (E077–E078). That is not a nuclear-weapons program, but it creates genuine safeguards and fuel-cycle responsibilities whose independent oversight and operational safety are not demonstrated here. The broad anti-war speech is favorable rhetoric but not nuclear-specific, while civilian firearms restrictions fall outside this chapter because the dossier supplies no credible existential-risk pathway. The safeguards evidence establishes institutional review rather than violations or exceptional performance. A higher score is therefore unwarranted without demonstrated consequential improvement in nuclear oversight or cyber implementation. A lower score is also unwarranted: the record contains direct, responsible formal choices and no evidence of nuclear coercion, proliferation, reckless threats, or unsafe crisis behavior.","lower_anchor_rejected":"Direct cybersecurity institution-building and responsible formal diplomacy outweigh the unresolved but not demonstrably reckless submarine exposure.","higher_anchor_rejected":"The record lacks multiple independent source types demonstrating consequential nuclear or cyber risk reduction.","manual_review_required":false,"manual_review_reason_type":null,"manual_review_reason":"Additional weak areas: 1B.3 operational control and safety; 1B.6 proliferation enforcement; 1B.7 independent expertise; 1B.10 demonstrated end-state improvement; Partially supported but weak lenses: 1B.3, 1B.7","chapter_specific":[{"field":"chapter_rubric_version","value":"chapter_1b_v5"},{"field":"exposure_profile","value":"limited_exposure_with_choices: safeguarded nuclear sector, nuclear-propulsion submarine program, and national cyber governance"},{"field":"trajectory","value":"modestly improving institutionally, with unresolved submarine safeguards and implementation outcomes"},{"field":"absolute_scale_classification","value":"generally_responsible_management"}]}
{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-population-top20-production-v2:2023:CHN:15833","iso3":"CHN","ruler_name":"Xi Jinping","score_1_to_10":3.5,"confidence_score":78.0,"plausible_score_range":{"lower":3.0,"upper":5.0},"decisive_positive_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E075","explanation":"Xi directly issued responsible nuclear-use rhetoric during the target year."}],"decisive_negative_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E058","explanation":"Technical analysis documents one of the largest and fastest nuclear modernization campaigns."},{"evidence_id":"E077","explanation":"Target-year evidence shows several hundred silos and a major ICBM-launcher buildup under Xi’s military authority."}],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"Xi inherited a nuclear arsenal and long-standing no-first-use posture; strategic rivalry and Taiwan tensions were external pressures, but expansion choices remained within his top military authority.","ruler_attribution":"Strong: Xi held the leading party, state, and military offices; sustained modernization and strategic diplomacy fall within formal and practical authority.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E075","explanation":"Xi directly endorsed the principle that nuclear war must not be fought."},{"evidence_id":"E076","explanation":"China participated in a P5 nuclear-risk meeting."},{"evidence_id":"E059","explanation":"The source explicitly says silo construction does not conclusively establish launch-on-warning doctrine."}],"source_mix":"FAS and Arms Control Association technical assessments, UN records, and official Chinese statements; strong on expansion and rhetoric, weak on classified command practice.","structured_prior_summary":"The available package contains five FAS-derived 2014 LF facts, including a 250-warhead inherited baseline and no target-year signals; they frame exposure only and do not determine direction.","chapter_rationale":"Overall, Xi’s record materially increased nuclear risk despite responsible declaratory language, scoring 3.5. China