{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-population-top20-production-v2:2023:DEU:16985","iso3":"DEU","ruler_name":"Olaf Scholz","score_1_to_10":7.0,"confidence_score":76.0,"plausible_score_range":{"lower":6.0,"upper":8.0},"decisive_positive_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E046","explanation":"Scholz directly articulated and applied an escalation-avoidance approach during the Ukraine crisis."},{"evidence_id":"E050","explanation":"Germany participated in the target-year CTBT entry-into-force process."}],"decisive_negative_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E048","explanation":"The government maintained nuclear-sharing capability rather than materially reducing nuclear exposure."}],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"Scholz inherited NATO nuclear sharing and faced Russia’s war and threats; coalition government and alliance commitments constrained unilateral choice.","ruler_attribution":"Direct for Scholz’s crisis rhetoric and cabinet leadership; shared with coalition ministers, parliament, NATO, and professional agencies for broader policy.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E047","explanation":"Observer participation and disarmament language coexisted with preserving NATO nuclear sharing."},{"evidence_id":"E048","explanation":"F-35 procurement explicitly retained nuclear-delivery capability."}],"source_mix":"Official German strategy, speech, procurement, and disarmament records plus CTBTO material; transparent but official-source heavy.","structured_prior_summary":"The available local package contains no LF/LS facts or signals and contributes no direction.","chapter_rationale":"Overall, Scholz managed nuclear and broader strategic risk responsibly but inconsistently, scoring 7.0. He directly emphasized avoiding unintended escalation and unilateral haste while supporting Ukraine, a meaningful crisis choice under severe Russian nuclear pressure (E046). His government condemned nuclear threats, participated in test-ban diplomacy, and continued arms-control and emerging-technology work (