{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-population-top20-production-v2:2023:EGY:16233","iso3":"EGY","ruler_name":"Abdel Fattah el-Sisi","score_1_to_10":6.0,"confidence_score":64.0,"plausible_score_range":{"lower":5.0,"upper":7.0},"decisive_positive_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E038","explanation":"Egypt ratified the Convention on Nuclear Safety during 2023."}],"decisive_negative_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E037","explanation":"Formal physical-protection and waste-governance gaps remained as the program expanded."}],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"Sisi inherited safeguards, CTBT non-ratification, and the Russian-backed El Dabaa project; the plant was still under construction.","ruler_attribution":"Strong formal presidential responsibility for national treaty and nuclear-program policy; technical execution is shared with regulators and the Russian vendor.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E037","explanation":"Egypt remained outside major physical-protection and waste conventions."},{"evidence_id":"E039","explanation":"CTBT non-ratification persisted through the target year."},{"evidence_id":"E035","explanation":"Russian lifecycle fuel dependence creates strategic and operational exposure."}],"source_mix":"IAEA and UN legal records plus specialist nuclear-industry synthesis; authoritative on formal status, limited on independent performance.","structured_prior_summary":"The available local package contains no LF/LS facts or signals and contributes no direction.","chapter_rationale":"Overall, Sisi’s record shows a concrete but incomplete improvement in civilian nuclear governance, scoring 6.0. Egypt advanced construction licensing for the El Dabaa power project and, most importantly, ratified the Convention on Nuclear Safety in September 2023 (E036, E038). That target-year treaty act is attributable to the state under Sisi’s presidential authority and supports a numeric score. The favorable step is offset by important inherited and continuing weaknesses: Egypt remained outside major physical-protection and spent-fuel conventions, had not ratified the nuclear-test-ban treaty, and depended heavily on Russia for El Dabaa fuel and used-fuel services (E035, E037, E039). The sources document legal status and construction milestones rather than independent regulatory performance, emergency readiness, or safe operation; Egypt still had no operating reactors in 2023 (E040). A higher score is rejected because a single safety-convention ratification and project milestones do not demonstrate consequential, durable risk reduction. A lower score is rejected because the ratification was a meaningful target-period improvement and no weapons program, nuclear threat, or reckless crisis conduct is shown.","lower_anchor_rejected":"The 2023 safety-convention ratification was a real improvement and no severe risk-increasing conduct is shown.","higher_anchor_rejected":"No independently verified implementation or broad treaty strengthening supports the 7–8 range.","manual_review_required":false,"manual_review_reason_type":null,"manual_review_reason":"Additional weak areas: 1B.2 responsible rhetoric; 1B.3 operational safety performance; 1B.5 nuclear shielding; 1B.7 independent expertise; 1B.8 crisis management; 1B.9 other catastrophic risks; Partially supported but weak lenses: 1B.3","chapter_specific":[{"field":"chapter_rubric_version","value":"chapter_1b_v5"},{"field":"exposure_profile","value":"limited_exposure_with_choices: first commercial nuclear plant under construction, Russian fuel-cycle dependence, and treaty-policy decisions"},{"field":"trajectory","value":"modestly improving through safety-convention ratification, with material protection and dependence gaps"},{"field":"absolute_scale_classification","value":"generally_responsible_management"}]}
{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-population-top20-production-v2:2023:ETH:16783","iso3":"ETH","ruler_name":"Abiy Ahmed","score_1_to_10":null,"confidence_score":26.0,"plausible_score_range":{"lower":1.0,"upper":10.0},"decisive_positive_evidence":[],"decisive_negative_evidence":[],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"Abiy inherited civilian cooperation law, treaty gaps, and limited technical capacity; the record does not isolate his use of corrective authority.","ruler_attribution":"Country and ministry context is available, but personal or formal executive responsibility for a directional 2023 choice is not established.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E004","explanation":"The national waste-management capacity gap is material context but lacks ruler-specific attribution."}],"source_mix":"IAEA legal and event records, an Ethiopian legal record, and a technical conference contribution; credible context but sparse attribution.","structured_prior_summary":"The available local package contains no LF/LS facts or signals and cannot support a score independently.","chapter_rationale":"The dossier does not support a defensible numeric Chapter 1B score for Abiy Ahmed. Ethiopia inherited a legal framework for peaceful nuclear cooperation, remained engaged in civilian nuclear planning, and had IAEA safeguards-related status, but those facts do not establish an attributable 2023 choice by Abiy (E001–E003). A contemporaneous technical contribution reported inadequate infrastructure, personnel, finance, technology, and law for radioactive-waste management (E004). That is a material national capacity warning, yet the source neither attributes the deficiency to a specific Abiy decision nor documents a target-year failure to use available corrective authority. Ethiopia’s non-party status for several nuclear safety and physical-protection conventions is similarly inherited country context rather than demonstrated 2023 governing conduct. There is no cited weapon posture, nuclear c