{"dossier_job_key":"dossier:2023-population-top20-production-v2:2023:IDN:16200","iso3":"IDN","ruler_name":"Joko Widodo","score_1_to_10":6.5,"confidence_score":58.0,"plausible_score_range":{"lower":5.5,"upper":7.5},"decisive_positive_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E006","explanation":"The government directly advocated a regional restraint mechanism during the target year."},{"evidence_id":"E007","explanation":"Indonesia formally prioritized disarmament and legally binding negative security assurances."}],"decisive_negative_evidence":[],"inherited_baseline_and_constraints":"Indonesia had limited direct nuclear authority and operated through regional and multilateral diplomacy; opportunity was meaningful but narrow.","ruler_attribution":"Formal responsibility attaches through Indonesia’s foreign-policy apparatus under the president; personal authorship is not required for this chapter.","contrary_evidence":[{"evidence_id":"E006","explanation":"The warning and treaty appeal are rhetoric and diplomacy, not operational proof."},{"evidence_id":"E007","explanation":"The formal position does not establish domestic or international implementation."}],"source_mix":"One contemporaneous AP report and one primary foreign-ministry statement; high relevance but narrow source diversity.","structured_prior_summary":"The available local package contains no LF/LS facts or signals and adds no direction.","chapter_rationale":"Overall, Joko Widodo’s government exercised a limited but clearly responsible nuclear-risk choice in 2023, scoring 6.5. Indonesia’s foreign minister warned that Southeast Asia was one miscalculation from catastrophe and urged nuclear-armed states to accept the regional nuclear-weapon-free-zone framework (E006). In a formal Non-Proliferation Treaty forum, Indonesia also made global disarmament and legally binding assurances against nuclear attack explicit priorities (E007). Foreign policy is a national executive responsibility, so these are attributable government choice